          "question": "I have been on a proton pump inhibitor for six years. Is long-term use a concern, what does guidance describe about review, and how is reflux coded?",
          "answer": "**Is long-term use a concern?** The guidance I could find does not describe the specific risks of six years of proton pump inhibitor (PPI) use, so I can't say how strong the evidence is for any of them. The searches returned nothing on fractures, kidney disease, vitamin B12, magnesium, infection, or dementia with PPIs, and I won't fill that gap from memory. One documented effect is that long-term acid suppression can reduce iron absorption, though the passage I found doesn't say how common this is (*Iron-deficiency anaemia: reference overview*). PPIs can also reduce how well some other medicines are absorbed, such as levothyroxine, so tell any clinician who prescribes you other medicines about your long-term use (*Hypothyroidism: which test comes first, and the monitoring interval after a change*).\n\n**What guidance says about review:** The reflux guideline says long-term acid suppression should be continued \"at the lowest effective intensity with periodic review, rather than renewed indefinitely without reassessment.\" It names three situations where long-term treatment may be needed: erosive oesophagitis, Barrett's oesophagus, and symptoms returning when the medicine is withdrawn (*GERD: empiric initial management and the alarm features that change it*). The guideline does not set a review interval. None of the retrieved sources disagreed about PPIs specifically.\n\n**How reflux is coded:** In ICD-10-CM, the US version of the classification, gastro-oesophageal reflux disease is category K21, within the digestive chapter (*Coding gastroesophageal reflux disease: K21 and the esophagitis distinction*; *ICD-10-CM Chapter 11*). The main distinction is whether oesophagitis is present, and if so whether bleeding is documented. Oesophagitis is a finding from an examination, usually an endoscopy, so a coder cannot infer it from symptom severity, the prescribed medicine, or how long the condition has lasted. Long-term drug therapy has its own status code, but the retrieved passages did not give its number. Coding is done by trained coders working from the full record.\n\n**What to ask your clinician:** Ask whether you still have a clear reason for the PPI, whether it is still the lowest effective dose, and when it was last reviewed. Do not stop or change the medicine without medical advice.\n\n**If you get chest pain,** especially with breathlessness, sweating, or pain spreading to the arm, jaw, or back, call emergency services immediately. Do not assume it is reflux (*Red-flag chest pain*).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
